iority exception). |\n138\t| B-E002 → B-E003 | Rules 12/13 | Llama Stack provider wiring builds against the connection-registration API/CR surface. |\n139\t| B-E002 → B-E004 | Rules 12/13 | Dashboard connection UI builds against the connection-registration API/CR surface. |\n140\t| B-E005 blocked by all branch-B Implementations | Rule 11 | docs-authoring always last within the branch. |\n141\t\n142\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n143\t\n144\t| HLR | Priority | Main-plan coverage | Branch A (managed) | Branch B (external) |\n145\t|-----|----------|--------------------|--------------------|---------------------|\n146\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 (Investigation) | — | — |\n147\t| HLR-2 | P0 | — | A-E002, A-E003, A-E004, A-E005 | B-E002 |\n148\t| HLR-3 | P1 | — | A-E006 | B-E003 |\n149\t| HLR-4 | P1 | — | A-E007 | B-E004 |\n150\t| HLR-5 | P2 | — | A-E008 | B-E005 |\n151\t\n152\tEvery HLR is covered in the main plan (HLR-1) or in **both** conditional\n153\tbranches (HLR-2 through HLR-5), matching the strategy's explicit conditional\n154\tphrasing (\"Depending on that decision\", \"the chosen vector store\", \"whichever\n155\tposture ships\"). No orphaned requirements.\n156\t\n157\tNote on HLR-5 priority: the docs epics are P1, not P2, because the\n158\t`docs-authoring` priority exception derives priority from the strategy\n159\tfrontmatter (`priority: Major` → P1) rather than from the P2 HLR.\n160\t\n161\t## Component & Team Mappings\n162\t\n163\t- **Milvus (vector database)** is not in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and has\n164\t  no architecture context file. Per the component-name constraint it is mapped\n165\t  to the closest parent-level canonical component **Llama Stack** (the RAG\n166\t  stack it serves), owned by **RHOAI GenAI**, for the provisioning epics\n167\t  (A-E002/A-E003/A-E004). The deployment epic (A-E005) is assigned to\n168\t  **Platform Operator** / **RHOAI Core** because the strategy makes the\n169\t  Platform Operator the deployment vehicle.\n170\t- **Platform Operator** is a canonical component but has **no architecture\n171\t  context file**, so its in-platform status could not be verified from the\n172\t  inventory. The strategy explicitly treats it as the existing deployment\n173\t  vehicle (\"deploy … through the Platform Operator\"), so it is treated as\n174\t  in-platform rather than generating a provisioning chain; see Health\n175\t  Warnings. Owner team **RHOAI Core** is assumed (the strategy provides no\n176\t  Impacted Teams table).\n177\t- **Documentation** epic team assumed **RHOAI Documentation** (no team table\n178\t  in strategy).\n179\t- **Llama Stack** → RHOAI GenAI and **Dashboard** → RHOAI Dashboard per their\n180\t  architecture context files.\n181\t\n182\t## New Component Detection (Step 2)\n183\t\n184\t- **Milvus**: not in architecture context → full onboarding chain generated\n185\t  per Rule 20 for branch A: `repo-onboarding` (A-E002) + `license-validation`\n186\t  (A-E003) parallel start → `konflux-onboarding` (A-E004, after repo) →\n187\t  consumed by the operator implementation (A-E005, after license-validation;\n188\t  parallel with konflux). No separate Investigation epic for dependency\n189\t  availability: Milvus is an established OSS project with a public upstream,\n190\t  so availability is not in doubt — only posture is, and that is E001's scope.\n191\t- **Branch B** provisions no new component: it connects to externally operated\n192\t  stores, so no onboarding chain applies.\n193\t\n194\t## Staff Engineer Input\n195\t\n196\tNone present in the strategy — no overrides to log.\n197\t\n198\t## Health Warnings\n199\t\n200\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n201\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then Acceptance\n202\t   Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from\n203\t   architecture context (Llama Stack → RHOAI GenAI, Dashboard → RHOAI\n204\t   Dashboard) and assumed for Platform Operator (RHOAI Core) and\n205\t   Documentation (RHOAI Documentation). Epic acceptance criteria were derived\n206\t   from HLR text rather than strategy ACs.\n207\t2. **Platform Operator lacks an architecture context file** (non-blocking\n208\t   scope watch): per the platform-inventory rule it is not verifiably \"in the\n209\t   platform\". Because the strategy asserts it as the existing deployment\n210\t   vehicle, it was decomposed as an existing component rather than generating\n211\t   a Rule 20 provisioning chain for the operator itself. If the operator's\n212\t   extension surface does not support third-party stateful components, branch\n213\t   A's A-E005 scope grows — verify during implementation. Signal 9\n214\t   (architecture_claims) is scored -1 on Platform Operator epics for this\n215\t   reason.\n216\t3. **Docs priority vs HLR-5**: HLR-5 is P2 in the strategy, but docs epics\n217\t   carry P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter\n218\t   `priority: Major` → P1). Flagged so the reviewer does not read it as a\n219\t   priority inversion.\n220\t4. **Stateful storage scope trap** (watch, not a flag): the managed posture\n221\t   implies operator-managed persistent storage for Milvus. The strategy's\n222\t   NFRs are absent, so storage sizing/limits are left as implementation\n223\t   scope; A-E008 (docs) must document limits explicitly per HLR-5.\n224\t\n225\t## Tiered Delivery\n226\t\n227\tNot applicable in the Rule-16/17 sense: there is no external dependency with\n228\tuncertain timing (Milvus is OSS productized by us; external stores in branch\n229\tB are customer-operated). The strategy-level fork is handled by conditional\n230\tbranches instead: no partial user value is deliverable before the posture\n231\tdecision (HLR-2 through HLR-5 are all posture-conditional), so E001 gates all\n232\tdownstream work. Within each branch, work proceeds in parallel per the DAG\n233\tabove.\n234\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-904a21329