{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb63c4f54264c89a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9007\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'critical_path_length: 1 in the decomposition summary frontmatter reflects\n10\t    the main plan only; the effective delivery critical path (4-5 epics) lives inside\n11\t    the conditional branches. The summary body explains this, but the bare frontmatter\n12\t    number could be misread at a glance.'\n13\terror: null\n14\t---\n15\t## Review Summary\n16\t\n17\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n18\tRecommendation: accept\n19\t\n20\t## Criterion Details\n21\t\n22\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n23\tAll five HLRs are mapped. HLR-1 (P0) is covered by the main-plan Investigation E001. HLR-2 through HLR-5 are covered in **both** conditional branches (A: managed, B: external), which correctly matches the strategy's explicit conditional phrasing (\"Depending on that decision\", \"whichever posture ships\"). The traceability matrix is present and complete. Priority inheritance is correct: E001, which gates all P0 work, is P0. The docs epics (A-E008, B-E005) carry P1 rather than the P2 of HLR-5 — this is the documented docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1), not a priority inversion, and the decomposition flags it proactively. No priority collapse: every epic maps to exactly one HLR, so deferrable lower-priority work is independently plannable.\n24\t\n25\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n26\tEvery edge in the summary DAG diagram was cross-checked against epic frontmatter `dependencies`, in both directions — all match, with no cycles and no references to nonexistent epics. Diagram arrows run dependency → dependent, consistent with frontmatter. Implementation-type parallelism was enumerated for every pair: no `license-validation → repo-onboarding` edge (Ru